---
import { Picture } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import bottleCaps from '../assets/images/bottle-caps.jpg';
import precisionMachining from '../assets/images/precision-machining.jpg';
---

<BaseLayout
    title="Capping Chuck Types: Magnetic, Friction, Pneumatic, Vacuum"
    description="The four capping chuck types explained simply: magnetic hysteresis, friction, pneumatic, and vacuum cap chucks. How each one works, specs, and which caps each one fits."
    canonical="https://cappingchucks.com/chuck-types/"
    ogTitle="Capping Chuck Types | CappingChucks.com"
    ogDescription="Magnetic, friction, pneumatic, and vacuum capping chucks. How each type works and when to use it."
    schemaType="TechArticle"
    breadcrumb="Capping Chuck Types"
>
    <PageHero
        title="Capping Chuck Types"
        crumb="Chuck Types"
        answerLabel="The short version"
        image={precisionMachining}
        alt="Metal lathe turning a precision steel component with coolant feed in a machining workshop"
        caption="Machining a metal part on a lathe. Photo: Pexels"
    >
        <p>There are four main capping chuck types: magnetic, friction, pneumatic, and vacuum. Pick one based on your line speed, your cap, and how often you switch cap types.</p>
    </PageHero>

    <main>
        <section id="classifications" class="section">
            <div class="wrap">
                <h2>How each chuck type works</h2>
                <div class="cards cards-2">
                    <div class="card card-blue" id="magnetic">
                        <span class="pill">Magnetic</span>
                        <h3>Magnetic hysteresis chucks</h3>
                        <p>Magnets pass the turning force from the motor to the cap across a small gap. Most use permanent magnets, and you set torque by turning an adjustment ring. A few electric models set it with coil current from the PLC. When the cap reaches the set torque, the magnets slip without touching. Nothing rubs, so nothing wears out.</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>No rubbing clutch parts</li>
                            <li>Torque holds steady over millions of caps</li>
                            <li>Set with an adjustment ring, or the PLC on electric models</li>
                            <li>Common on fast rotary cappers</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p class="mt-3">These chucks are the heart of <a href="/capping-headsets/">magnetic capping headsets</a>.</p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="card card-red" id="friction">
                        <span class="pill">Mechanical</span>
                        <h3>Friction chucks</h3>
                        <p>A spring presses clutch plates together. The plates pass the turning force by friction. When the cap is tight, the plates slip. To change torque, tighten or loosen the spring with a threaded collar or shims. Best at lower speeds, where slipping plates don't get too hot.</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Spring pressure sets the torque</li>
                            <li>Clutch plates are a wear part</li>
                            <li>Adjusted with shims or a threaded collar</li>
                            <li>Good value on inline and slower rotary lines</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <div class="card card-yellow" id="pneumatic">
                        <span class="pill">Air</span>
                        <h3>Pneumatic chucks</h3>
                        <p>Air pressure controls how hard the chuck grips and how much torque it applies. An air cylinder closes jaws or a flexible collar around the cap. A PLC can change the air settings for each product, so you can switch cap types without changing parts.</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Torque set by air valves the PLC controls</li>
                            <li>Saved settings for each product</li>
                            <li>Air-driven jaws or collar</li>
                            <li>Best for lines that run many different products</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <div class="card card-green" id="vacuum">
                        <span class="pill">Suction</span>
                        <h3>Vacuum cap chucks</h3>
                        <p>A vacuum cap chuck holds the cap with suction instead of squeezing it. A small port in the chuck pulls air out and draws the cap up into the chuck. The cap sits centered over the bottle before the spindle comes down. Used for thin or light caps that squeezing would bend out of round.</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Suction holds thin and delicate caps</li>
                            <li>Vacuum comes from a venturi (air-powered) or a pump</li>
                            <li>Keeps caps from bending at high speed</li>
                            <li>Used on flat caps and light caps</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="grip" class="section section-alt">
            <div class="wrap">
                <h2>Chuck body styles</h2>
                <p class="lead prose-col">The four types above are about how torque is controlled. Separately, <a href="/">bottle capping chucks</a> differ in how the body holds the cap.</p>
                <div class="cards cards-2">
                    <div class="card" id="one-piece">
                        <h3>One-piece (rigid) chucks</h3>
                        <p>A solid aluminum or stainless steel cup with a rubber or urethane <a href="#inserts">insert</a> inside. The insert is sized to one cap. This is the simplest and most common chuck.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card" id="jaw">
                        <h3>Jaw chucks</h3>
                        <p>Three or four metal or plastic jaws close around the cap, driven by springs, cams, or air. Jaws can grip caps with odd shapes, tabs, or spouts that a plain cup can't hold.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card" id="flexible">
                        <h3>Flexible (bladder) chucks</h3>
                        <p>A rubber sleeve inflates with air and squeezes around the cap. It adjusts to small size differences, so one chuck covers caps that vary a little. Collet chucks do the same job with slotted fingers.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card" id="press-on">
                        <h3>Press-on heads</h3>
                        <p>A flat pad that pushes a snap-on cap straight down without turning. Used for overcaps, snap caps, and plugs on <a href="/capping-machines/#snap-capper">snap cappers</a> and chuck cappers.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="inserts" class="section">
            <div class="wrap prose-col">
                <h2>Chuck inserts and friction rings</h2>
                <p>The insert is the part that actually touches the cap. Most are a ring or cup of rubber or urethane pressed into the chuck body. Some are called friction rings or friction bands. The inside is either smooth or ribbed to match the <a href="/glossary/#knurling">knurling</a> on the cap.</p>
                <ul class="bullets">
                    <li><strong>Material.</strong> Inserts are made of rubber or urethane. Ask the chuck maker which suits your cap and cleaning chemicals. On food and drink lines, use a material that meets FDA food-contact rules, such as <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-B/part-177/subpart-C/section-177.2600" rel="noopener" target="_blank">21 CFR 177.2600</a> for rubber.</li>
                    <li><strong>Hardness.</strong> Measured in <a href="/glossary/#durometer">durometer</a> (Shore A). Inserts commonly come in about 40 to 80 Shore A, with 55 a common middle choice (<a href="https://www.kinexcappers.com/faq/about-liners.htm" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Kinex</a>). Softer inserts grip better but wear faster. Harder inserts last longer but can let the cap slip.</li>
                    <li><strong>Size.</strong> The insert is sized to the cap's outside width across the ridges. Measure the cap, not the bottle. See <a href="/cap-sizes/#sizing-a-chuck">sizing a chuck to a cap</a>.</li>
                    <li><strong>When to replace.</strong> Replace inserts when they look shiny, cracked, or flared, when caps start slipping or scuffing, or when <a href="/cap-torque/">torque</a> spread goes up. Track insert changes by cap count so you learn your own interval.</li>
                </ul>
                <div class="note">
                    <strong>Tip:</strong> Replace every insert on a turret at the same time. Mixing worn and new inserts makes torque vary from spindle to spindle.
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section id="applications" class="section section-alt">
            <div class="wrap">
                <h2>Which chuck for which cap</h2>

                <figure class="mb-8">
                    <Picture
                        src={bottleCaps}
                        alt="Assorted plastic closure caps in various sizes and colors used across beverage, dairy, and pharmaceutical packaging"
                        class="w-full h-56 sm:h-72 object-cover rounded-3xl border-[3px] border-ink"
                        widths={[600, 900, 1200]}
                        sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px"
                        formats={['avif', 'webp']}
                        quality={80}
                        loading="lazy"
                    />
                    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-base text-ink-soft">Caps come in many sizes, materials, and tamper band styles. Photo: Pexels</figcaption>
                </figure>

                <div class="cards cards-3">
                    <div class="card">
                        <span class="pill">PCO-1881-T</span>
                        <h3>28 mm <a href="https://www.isbt.com/resources/isbt-threadspecs" rel="noopener" target="_blank">PCO 1881</a> tethered caps</h3>
                        <p>Tethered caps stay attached to the bottle. Since July 3, 2024, the <a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/904/oj" rel="noopener" target="_blank">EU Single-Use Plastics Directive</a> requires them on single-use plastic drink bottles up to 3 liters. Most cappers need new chuck cones shaped to clear the hinge and tether (<a href="https://www.arol.com/news-events/tethered-caps-change-is-coming" rel="noopener" target="_blank">AROL</a>).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card">
                        <span class="pill">HDPE-38</span>
                        <h3>38 mm dairy and juice caps</h3>
                        <p>Wide plastic caps for milk, juice, and smoothies. They need a strong grip on the <a href="/glossary/#knurling">knurling</a>. Guide charts give 17 to 26 in-lb (about 1.9 to 2.9 N&middot;m) for 38 mm caps. Check this against the cap maker's spec and the foil seal target. See the <a href="/cap-torque/#chart">cap torque chart</a>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card">
                        <span class="pill">ROPP</span>
                        <h3>Roll-on pilfer-proof caps</h3>
                        <p>Aluminum caps for spirits, wine, olive oil, and medicine syrups. The <a href="/capping-headsets/#ropp">ROPP headset</a> uses rollers to press threads into the cap. Roller shape must match the glass threads exactly. See <a href="/ropp-capping/">ROPP capping</a>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card">
                        <span class="pill">CRC</span>
                        <h3>Child-resistant caps</h3>
                        <p>Push-and-turn and squeeze-and-turn caps for medicine and cleaners. The chuck pushes down and turns at the same time to lock the cap without overtightening the inner seal. In the U.S., these are covered by <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-16/chapter-II/subchapter-E/part-1700" rel="noopener" target="_blank">16 CFR Part 1700</a>. Use the cap maker's torque spec and test the finished package.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card">
                        <span class="pill">Sport</span>
                        <h3>Sport and pull-push caps</h3>
                        <p>Flip-top and pull-push caps for water, sports drinks, and personal care. The chuck grips the cap body without touching the spout. The inside of the chuck has extra room for the spout and hinge.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="card">
                        <span class="pill">Trigger</span>
                        <h3>Trigger sprayer overcaps</h3>
                        <p>Overcaps for spray cleaners and garden products. These snap on instead of screwing on. A <a href="#press-on">press-on head</a> pushes straight down with a set force (<a href="/glossary/#top-load">top load</a>) instead of turning.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="section">
            <div class="wrap">
                <h2>Keep reading</h2>
                <ul class="related">
                    <li><a href="/capping-headsets/">Magnetic capping headsets</a></li>
                    <li><a href="/cap-sizes/">Neck finish codes like 38-400</a></li>
                    <li><a href="/capping-machines/">Capping machine types</a></li>
                    <li><a href="/troubleshooting/">Troubleshooting</a></li>
                    <li><a href="/oem-compatibility/">OEM compatibility</a></li>
                    <li><a href="/glossary/">Glossary</a></li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>
    </main>
</BaseLayout>
